      "name": "Core Syntax",
      "scenarios": [
        {
          "id": "cpp-range-for-copy",
          "title": "The loop that modifies copies",
          "situation": "A normalization pass iterates a vector of readings with a range-for loop, but the values in the vector never change.",
          "prompt": "Why does the mutation not stick?",
          "evidence": [
            [
              "norm.cpp",
              "for (auto r : readings) { r = (r - min) / (max - min); }  // vector unchanged"
            ]
          ],
          "choices": [
            "auto deduces a by-value copy \u2014 each iteration mutates a temporary; use auto& to bind the actual element",
            "Range-for loops are const by default \u2014 add the mutable keyword",
            "auto cannot deduce numeric types in loops \u2014 write double r explicitly",
            "The assignment needs std::move \u2014 r = std::move(...) instead"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "why": "In for (auto r : readings), r is a fresh copy of each element; writes to r die with the iteration. for (auto& r : readings) binds a reference to the element itself, so mutations persist. Use const auto& when only reading, to avoid copies of expensive types.",
          "hint": "Is r the element, or a copy of the element?",
          "objectives": [
            "Range-based for",
            "auto deduction",
            "References"
          ],
          "mins": 5
        },
        {
          "id": "cpp-most-vexing",
          "title": "The object that is a function",
          "situation": "A developer writes Widget w(); expecting a default-constructed Widget, then gets bizarre errors when calling w.reset().",
          "prompt": "What did the declaration actually declare?",
          "evidence": [
            [
              "widget.cpp",
              "Widget w();   // not an object!\nw.reset();  // error: request for member in non-class type"
            ]
          ],
          "choices": [
            "The most vexing parse: w is declared as a function taking no arguments and returning Widget \u2014 use Widget w{} or Widget w;",
            "Parentheses are required for construction \u2014 the compiler needs a flag to accept it",
            "Widget has no default constructor \u2014 this declares an abstract placeholder",
            "w is a function pointer \u2014 call it as w()->reset()"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "why": "C++ grammar prefers to parse Widget w(); as a function declaration (returning Widget, taking no parameters) rather than a variable definition. Brace initialization Widget w{} unambiguously defines an object \u2014 and additionally forbids narrowing conversions.",
          "hint": "When a statement can be parsed as either a declaration or a definition, which does the grammar prefer?",
          "objectives": [
            "Most vexing parse",
            "Uniform initialization"
          ],
          "mins": 5
        },
        {
          "id": "cpp-narrowing",
          "title": "The initializer that refuses to compile",
          "situation": "A config struct initialized with braces fails to compile when a double is passed for an int field, while the parenthesized version compiled silently.",
          "prompt": "What protection does brace initialization provide?",
          "evidence": [
            [
              "cfg.cpp",
              "struct Limits { int max_conn; };\ndouble d = 100.5;\nLimits l1{d};      // error: narrowing\nLimits l2(d);      // compiles, truncates silently"
            ]
          ],
          "choices": [
            "Braced initialization forbids narrowing conversions \u2014 the error caught a lossy double-to-int conversion that parentheses would silently allow",
            "Braces zero-initialize \u2014 l1.max_conn is 0, not 100",
            "Uniform initialization only works for aggregates \u2014 Limits needs a constructor",
            "The error is a compiler bug \u2014 both forms must behave identically"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "why": "List initialization (braces) makes narrowing conversions \u2014 those that can lose information, like double\u2192int \u2014 ill-formed. Parenthesized and = initialization permit them silently. Preferring braces turns accidental truncation into a compile-time error at the point of the mistake.",
          "hint": "Which initialization form did the standards committee make reject lossy conversions?",
          "objectives": [
            "List initialization",
            "Narrowing conversions"
          ],
          "mins": 5
        },
        {
          "id": "cpp-const-member",
          "title": "The getter that will not compile on const objects",
          "situation": "A getter works on regular objects but fails to compile when called through a const reference, breaking a read-only API.",
          "prompt": "Why does the const context reject the call?",
          "evidence": [
            [
              "store.cpp",
              "class Store {\n    int count() { return n; }  // not const\n};\nvoid report(const Store& s) { std::cout << s.count(); }  // error"
            ]
          ],
          "choices": [
            "count() is not marked const, so it cannot be called on a const object \u2014 add const after the parameter list",
            "const references cannot call any member functions \u2014 take Store by value",
            "std::cout is not const-correct \u2014 the error is in the stream, not the getter",
            "Getters must be static \u2014 make count() a static member"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "why": "A member function without the trailing const is treated as potentially mutating, so the type system forbids calling it on const objects. Marking it int count() const promises read-only access and makes it callable in const contexts. Const-correctness is viral: one non-const getter poisons every const caller.",
          "hint": "What does the compiler assume a member function might do unless you promise otherwise?",
          "objectives": [
            "const correctness",
            "const member functions"
          ],
          "mins": 5
        },
        {
          "id": "cpp-using-namespace-header",
          "title": "The header that pollutes everything",
          "situation": "A widely included header has using namespace std; at the top. Unrelated .cpp files start failing with ambiguous symbol errors after an unrelated library upgrade.",
          "prompt": "Why is using namespace in headers dangerous?",
          "evidence": [
            [
              "util.h",
              "using namespace std;  // in a header: affects every includer\n// ... later, someone's `count` collides with std::count"
            ]
          ],
          "choices": [
            "The directive dumps every std name into the global namespace of every translation unit that includes the header \u2014 use explicit std:: qualification or targeted using-declarations in headers",
            "Headers cannot contain using directives \u2014 it is a compile error being suppressed",
            "std:: names are macros \u2014 they expand differently per translation unit",
            "The upgrade renamed std::count \u2014 pin the library version instead"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "why": "A using-directive in a header is transitive: everyone who includes it (directly or indirectly) gets the entire namespace injected, inviting collisions like a local count vs std::count and silently changing overload resolution. Keep using-directives in .cpp files after all includes, or qualify with std::.",
          "hint": "Who else is affected by a using-directive at the top of a header \u2014 just that header, or everyone who includes it?",
          "objectives": [
            "Namespaces",
            "Header hygiene",
            "using-directives"
          ],
          "mins": 5
        },
        {
          "id": "cpp-overload-conversion",
          "title": "The surprising overload",
          "situation": "Calling log(0) with overloads for log(long) and log(const std::string&) compiles but picks an unexpected overload after a refactor changed int overloads.",
          "prompt": "How does overload resolution rank the candidates?",
          "evidence": [
            [
              "log.cpp",
              "void log(long n);\nvoid log(const std::string& s);\nlog(0);  // calls log(long): exact-ish integral conversion beats user-defined conversion"
            ]
          ],
          "choices": [
            "Overload resolution prefers standard conversions (int\u2192long) over user-defined conversions (int\u2192std::string via constructor) \u2014 the ranking is exact match, promotion, conversion, user-defined",
            "The first declared overload always wins ties \u2014 reorder the declarations",
            "0 is a null pointer constant \u2014 it always selects pointer overloads",
            "Overloads are resolved at link time \u2014 the linker picked arbitrarily"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "why": "Overload resolution ranks implicit conversion sequences: exact match first, then integral/floating promotions, then other standard conversions (int\u2192long), and only then user-defined conversions (via a converting constructor to std::string). Understanding the ranking predicts which overload is chosen without trial and error.",
          "hint": "Which requires more work from the compiler: converting int to long, or constructing a std::string from an int?",
          "objectives": [
            "Overload resolution",
            "Implicit conversions"
          ],
          "mins": 7
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "oop-cpp",
      "name": "OOP in C++",
      "scenarios": [
        {
          "id": "cpp-virtual-dtor",
          "title": "The derived destructor that never runs",
          "situation": "Objects are deleted through a base-class pointer. Derived destructors never execute and resources leak, though the code compiles cleanly.",
          "prompt": "What is missing from the base class?",
          "evidence": [
            [
              "poly.cpp",
              "struct Base { ~Base() {} /* not virtual */ };\nstruct Derived : Base { std::vector<int> big; ~Derived() { /* releases */ } };\nBase* p = new Derived; delete p;  // Derived::~Derived never runs: UB"
            ]
          ],
          "choices": [
            "The base destructor must be virtual \u2014 without it, deleting through a base pointer is undefined behavior and skips derived cleanup",
            "Derived needs to redeclare the destructor as override \u2014 the base one is fine",
            "Delete must be called on the derived pointer type \u2014 store Derived* instead",
            "Destructors cannot be virtual \u2014 use a virtual close() method and call it manually"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "why": "Deleting a derived object through a base pointer without a virtual destructor is undefined behavior; in practice the derived destructor (and member destruction) is skipped, leaking resources. Rule: any class designed as a base with polymorphic use needs a virtual destructor \u2014 often virtual ~Base() = default.",
          "hint": "When delete runs through a Base*, how does it know which destructor code to execute?",
          "objectives": [
            "Virtual destructors",
            "Polymorphic deletion"
          ],
          "mins": 6
        },
        {
          "id": "cpp-slicing",
          "title": "The derived part that got sliced off",
          "situation": "A function takes a Base by value and is passed Derived objects. Virtual calls inside behave like the base, and derived data is gone.",
          "prompt": "What happened to the derived portion?",
          "evidence": [
            [
              "slice.cpp",
              "void process(Base b) { b.speak(); }  // takes Base BY VALUE\nDerived d; process(d);  // d sliced to its Base subobject; speak() is Base's"
            ]
          ],
          "choices": [
            "Object slicing: copying a Derived into a Base copies only the Base subobject \u2014 pass by reference (const Base&) to preserve polymorphism",
            "Virtual dispatch requires pointers \u2014 references do not dispatch virtually",
            "The copy constructor is implicitly deleted \u2014 the code should not compile",
            "Derived's vtable pointer is null after the copy \u2014 initialize it manually"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "why": "Copying a derived object into a base-typed variable slices off everything beyond the base subobject, including the dynamic type \u2014 virtual calls then resolve to Base. Passing by (const) reference or pointer keeps the complete object and its dynamic type intact. Slicing is silent and compiles without warnings.",
          "hint": "What fits in a Base-sized variable when you hand it a bigger Derived object?",
          "objectives": [
            "Object slicing",
            "Pass by reference",
            "Polymorphism"
          ],
          "mins": 6
        },
        {
          "id": "cpp-override-mismatch",
          "title": "The override that was not",
          "situation": "A derived class intends to override a base virtual function, but the base version keeps getting called. No compiler error appears.",
          "prompt": "Why did the override silently fail, and which keyword prevents this?",
          "evidence": [
            [
              "ovr.cpp",
              "struct Base { virtual void draw(int x); };\nstruct Derived : Base { virtual void draw(long x); };  // different param: hides, not overrides\nBase* p = new Derived; p->draw(1);  // calls Base::draw"
            ]
          ],
          "choices": [
            "draw(long) has a different signature, so it hides rather than overrides \u2014 marking it override makes the mismatch a compile error",
            "Virtual functions cannot be redeclared in derived classes \u2014 remove the declaration",
            "int and long are the same type on this platform \u2014 the bug is elsewhere",
            "The base pointer must be cast to Derived* for virtual dispatch"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "why": "Overriding requires an identical signature (plus compatible return types). draw(long) is a different function that hides the base name instead of overriding it. The override specifier makes the compiler verify the override \u2014 a signature mismatch becomes an error at the declaration, not a silent runtime surprise.",
          "hint": "For a derived function to override, what must match exactly \u2014 and what helps the compiler check that for you?",
          "objectives": [
            "override specifier",
            "Name hiding",
            "Virtual functions"
          ],
          "mins": 6
        },
        {
          "id": "cpp-ctor-virtual",
          "title": "The virtual call in the constructor",
          "situation": "A base constructor calls a virtual setup() method expecting the derived version, but the base version runs instead.",
          "prompt": "Why does the virtual call not dispatch to the derived class during construction?",
          "evidence": [
            [
              "ctor.cpp",
              "struct Base {\n    Base() { setup(); }            // calls Base::setup, NOT Derived::setup\n    virtual void setup() {...}\n};\nstruct Derived : Base { void setup() override {...} };"
            ]
          ],
          "choices": [
            "During base construction the object's dynamic type is Base \u2014 the derived part does not exist yet, so virtual calls resolve to the base version",
            "Constructors cannot call member functions \u2014 move the call after construction",
            "setup() must be pure virtual for dispatch to work in constructors",
            "The vtable is null until construction completes \u2014 virtual calls crash, not fall back"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "why": "While the base constructor runs, the derived members have not been constructed \u2014 the object's dynamic type is effectively the base. Virtual dispatch therefore resolves within the base. Calling a pure virtual from a constructor is even worse (undefined behavior). The fix: pass needed data as constructor parameters, or use a post-construction init step.",
          "hint": "What parts of the object exist at the moment the base constructor body runs?",
          "objectives": [
            "Virtual dispatch during construction",
            "Object lifetime"
          ],
          "mins": 7
        },
        {
          "id": "cpp-inherit-access",
          "title": "The private inheritance surprise",
          "situation": "A class inherits from a base with the default inheritance access, and suddenly public base methods are unreachable from outside.",
          "prompt": "What does the inheritance access specifier control?",
          "evidence": [
            [
              "inh.cpp",
              "class Cache : Store {  // class defaults to PRIVATE inheritance\n};\nCache c; c.lookup(\"k\");  // error: lookup is inaccessible"
            ]
          ],
          "choices": [
            "class defaults to private inheritance, making all inherited members private \u2014 write class Cache : public Store to model is-a",
            "Inheritance is always public in C++ \u2014 the error must come from lookup being private in Store",
            "Private inheritance is illegal \u2014 use composition instead, always",
            "The access specifier only affects data members, not functions"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "why": "The access specifier on inheritance caps the accessibility of inherited members: private inheritance (the default for class, unlike struct) makes public/protected base members private in the derived class. Public inheritance models is-a; private inheritance models implemented-in-terms-of and is rare \u2014 composition is usually clearer.",
          "hint": "What is the default inheritance access for class versus struct, and what does it do to inherited public members?",
          "objectives": [
            "Inheritance access",
            "public vs private inheritance"
          ],
          "mins": 5
        },
        {
          "id": "cpp-pure-virtual",
          "title": "The interface that cannot be built",
          "situation": "A team designs a plugin interface as an abstract base, but a developer tries to instantiate it directly and gets a compile error.",
          "prompt": "How do pure virtual functions define an interface, and what must a concrete plugin do?",
          "evidence": [
            [
              "plugin.cpp",
              "struct Plugin {\n    virtual void run() = 0;  // pure virtual: Plugin is abstract\n};\nPlugin p;  // error: cannot instantiate abstract class"
            ]
          ],
          "choices": [
            "A pure virtual function (= 0) makes the class abstract \u2014 concrete plugins must override every pure virtual to become instantiable",
            "= 0 deletes the function \u2014 derived classes cannot provide an implementation",
            "Abstract classes cannot have data members \u2014 remove them to instantiate",
            "The error means the vtable is missing \u2014 link against the plugin library"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "why": "= 0 declares a pure virtual function with no required implementation in the base, making the class abstract \u2014 it can be used as a pointer/reference type but never instantiated. A derived class becomes concrete only once it overrides all inherited pure virtuals. (A pure virtual can still have a definition that derived classes call explicitly.)",
          "hint": "What does = 0 say about who must provide the implementation?",
          "objectives": [
            "Pure virtual functions",
            "Abstract classes",
            "Interfaces"
          ],
          "mins": 5
        }
      ]
