      "id": "stl-containers",
      "name": "STL Containers",
      "scenarios": [
        {
          "id": "cpp-vector-invalidation",
          "title": "The iterator that died on push_back",
          "situation": "A loop holds an iterator into a vector while appending new elements. After a few iterations it reads garbage.",
          "prompt": "Why did the iterator become invalid?",
          "evidence": [
            [
              "vec.cpp",
              "auto it = v.begin();\nfor (int i = 0; i < 1000; i++) {\n    v.push_back(i);   // may reallocate\n    *it = i;          // UB after reallocation\n}"
            ]
          ],
          "choices": [
            "push_back can reallocate the vector's storage, invalidating all iterators, pointers, and references \u2014 refresh them after any mutating operation",
            "Iterators are single-use \u2014 a vector iterator cannot be dereferenced twice",
            "push_back invalidates only const_iterators \u2014 use a mutable iterator",
            "The loop bound is wrong \u2014 iterators expire after 512 uses"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "why": "A vector stores elements contiguously; when push_back exceeds capacity it allocates a bigger buffer and moves everything, leaving old iterators dangling. The rule: any operation that may reallocate (push_back, insert, reserve beyond capacity) invalidates iterators. Call v.reserve(n) up front when the final size is known, or re-acquire iterators after mutation.",
          "hint": "Where do the elements physically live after the vector outgrows its buffer?",
          "objectives": [
            "Iterator invalidation",
            "vector reallocation"
          ],
          "mins": 6
        },
        {
          "id": "cpp-map-bracket",
          "title": "The lookup that inserts",
