            ]
          ],
          "choices": [
            "operator[] default-constructs a value for missing keys and inserts it \u2014 use find() or at() for non-inserting lookup",
            "operator[] throws std::out_of_range on missing keys \u2014 the growth is from another thread",
            "Maps auto-vivify only for int values \u2014 string values are safe",
            "The insertion happens because counts is not const \u2014 const maps do not insert"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "why": "map::operator[] is specified to insert a value-initialized element when the key is absent \u2014 convenient for counting, surprising for lookups. For read-only access use find() (returns end() if missing) or at() (throws out_of_range). Note operator[] cannot even be called on a const map, which is a hint about its mutating nature.",
          "hint": "Is operator[] a const member function? What does that tell you about what it may do?",
