          "id": "cpp-erase-remove",
          "title": "The remove that removed nothing",
          "situation": "A cleanup pass calls std::remove to drop expired sessions from a vector, but size() is unchanged afterward.",
          "prompt": "Why did the vector not shrink?",
          "evidence": [
            [
              "sess.cpp",
              "std::remove(v.begin(), v.end(), expired);  // elements shifted, size unchanged!\n// v.erase(std::remove(...), v.end()); is the full idiom"
            ]
          ],
          "choices": [
            "std::remove only rearranges \u2014 it returns the new logical end; you must erase() that range (the erase-remove idiom) to actually shrink the container",
            "std::remove requires a sorted range \u2014 sort first",
            "remove works only on lists \u2014 vectors need manual loops",
            "The predicate form is std::remove_if \u2014 std::remove compares addresses"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "why": "Algorithms operate on iterator ranges, not containers \u2014 std::remove cannot change v's size, so it compacts the kept elements to the front and returns the new logical end. The erase-remove idiom, v.erase(std::remove(...), v.end()), erases the leftover tail. (C++20's std::erase(v, value) does both in one call.)",
          "hint": "Can an algorithm that only sees iterators change the container's size? What must it return instead?",
