  "lessons": [
    {
      "domain_id": "core-syntax",
      "title": "Core Syntax \u2014 Core concepts",
      "concepts": [
        "auto deduction: use const auto& when only reading, to avoid copies of expensive types.",
        "Most vexing parse: C++ grammar prefers to parse Widget w(); as a function declaration (returning Widget, taking no parameters) rather than a variable definition.",
        "Uniform initialization: list initialization (braces) makes narrowing conversions \u2014 those that can lose information, like double\u2192int \u2014 ill-formed. Brace initialization Widget w{} unambiguously defines an object \u2014 and additionally forbids narrowing conversions.",
        "List initialization: parenthesized and = initialization permit them silently."
      ],
      "example": {
        "lang": "c",
        "code": "using namespace std;  // in a header: affects every includer\n// ... later, someone's `count` collides with std::count",
        "walkthrough": "A using-directive in a header is transitive: everyone who includes it (directly or indirectly) gets the entire namespace injected, inviting collisions like a local count vs std::count and silently changing overload resolution. Keep using-directives in .cpp files after all includes, or qualify with std::."
      },
      "pitfalls": [
        "Common trap: const references cannot call any member functions.",
        "Common trap: uniform initialization only works for aggregates.",
        "Common trap: auto cannot deduce numeric types in loops.",
        "Common trap: the first declared overload always wins ties."
      ],
      "try_it": "Try it now in the Terminal tab \u2192"
    },
    {
      "domain_id": "oop-cpp",
      "title": "OOP in C++ \u2014 Core concepts",
      "concepts": [
        "Virtual destructors: = 0 declares a pure virtual function with no required implementation in the base, making the class abstract \u2014 it can be used as a pointer/reference type but never instantiated.",
        "Polymorphic deletion: rule: any class designed as a base with polymorphic use needs a virtual destructor \u2014 often virtual ~Base() = default.",
        "Object slicing: copying a derived object into a base-typed variable slices off everything beyond the base subobject, including the dynamic type \u2014 virtual calls then resolve to Base. While the base constructor runs, the derived members have not been constructed \u2014 the object's dynamic type is effectively the base.",
        "Passing by (const) reference or pointer keeps the complete object and its dynamic type intact. The fix: pass needed data as constructor parameters, or use a post-construction init step.",
        "override specifier: the override specifier makes the compiler verify the override \u2014 a signature mismatch becomes an error at the declaration, not a silent runtime surprise."
      ],
      "example": {
        "lang": "cpp",
        "code": "void process(Base b) { b.speak(); }  // takes Base BY VALUE\nDerived d; process(d);  // d sliced to its Base subobject; speak() is Base's",
        "walkthrough": "Copying a derived object into a base-typed variable slices off everything beyond the base subobject, including the dynamic type \u2014 virtual calls then resolve to Base. Slicing is silent and compiles without warnings."
      },
      "pitfalls": [
        "Common trap: setup() must be pure virtual for dispatch to work in constructors.",
        "Common trap: virtual functions cannot be redeclared in derived classes.",
        "Common trap: the access specifier only affects data members, not functions.",
        "Common trap: the base pointer must be cast to Derived* for virtual dispatch."
      ],
      "try_it": "Try it now in the Terminal tab \u2192"
    },
    {
      "domain_id": "stl-containers",
      "title": "STL Containers \u2014 Core concepts",
      "concepts": [
        "Iterator invalidation: algorithms operate on iterator ranges, not containers \u2014 std::remove cannot change v's size, so it compacts the kept elements to the front and returns the new logical end.",
        "vector reallocation: a vector stores elements contiguously; when push_back exceeds capacity it allocates a bigger buffer and moves everything, leaving old iterators dangling.",
        "Map::operator[] is specified to insert a value-initialized element when the key is absent \u2014 convenient for counting, surprising for lookups. Ordered map needs only operator<; unordered_map needs a hash (to pick a bucket) and equality (to resolve collisions).",
        "find vs at: for read-only access use find() (returns end() if missing) or at() (throws out_of_range).",
        "Erase-remove idiom: the erase-remove idiom, v.erase(std::remove(...), v.end()), erases the leftover tail."
      ],
      "example": {
        "lang": "cpp",
        "code": "std::remove(v.begin(), v.end(), expired);  // elements shifted, size unchanged!\n// v.erase(std::remove(...), v.end()); is the full idiom",
        "walkthrough": "Algorithms operate on iterator ranges, not containers \u2014 std::remove cannot change v's size, so it compacts the kept elements to the front and returns the new logical end. (C++20's std::erase(v, value) does both in one call.)."
      },
      "pitfalls": [
        "Common trap: operator[] throws std::out_of_range on missing keys.",
        "Common trap: a vector iterator cannot be dereferenced twice.",
        "Common trap: std::sort requires a total order.",
        "Common trap: std::remove requires a sorted range."
      ],
      "try_it": "Try it now in the Terminal tab \u2192"
    },
    {
      "domain_id": "memory-smart-pointers",
      "title": "Memory & Smart Pointers \u2014 Core concepts",
      "concepts": [
        "Exception safety: RAII ties resource release to object lifetime: the lock_guard's destructor runs when the scope exits for any reason \u2014 return, exception, or fall-through \u2014 so the mutex cannot be left locked.",
        "unique_ptr: the compiler may evaluate new Widget, then priority(), then the unique_ptr constructor \u2014 if priority() throws in between, the raw pointer is lost and leaks. std::make_unique performs the allocation directly into the smart pointer's control path, eliminating the window.",
        "Move semantics: std::move is a cast to an rvalue reference; the move constructor then steals the source's resources. (unique_lock adds deferred/try semantics when needed.).",
        "Shared_ptr destroys its object when the last owner goes away, but in a cycle every object has an owner inside the cycle \u2014 the count never drops to zero. weak_ptr holds a non-owning reference: it can lock() to a temporary shared_ptr when access is needed, and it does not keep the target alive."
      ],
      "example": {
        "lang": "cpp",
        "code": "take(std::unique_ptr<Widget>(new Widget), priority());\n// if priority() throws after new but before the unique_ptr is built: leak",
        "walkthrough": "The compiler may evaluate new Widget, then priority(), then the unique_ptr constructor \u2014 if priority() throws in between, the raw pointer is lost and leaks. std::make_unique performs the allocation directly into the smart pointer's control path, eliminating the window. It is also shorter and exception-safe by construction. (make_shared additionally fuses the object and control block into one allocation.)."
      },
      "pitfalls": [
        "Common trap: std::move destroys the source.",
        "Common trap: wrap the call in try/catch and delete manually in the catch block.",
        "Common trap: scalar delete works for arrays of trivial types.",
        "Common trap: delete on arrays leaks the count prefix."
      ],
      "try_it": "Try it now in the Terminal tab \u2192"
    },
    {
      "domain_id": "templates-lambdas",
      "title": "Templates & Lambdas \u2014 Core concepts",
      "concepts": [
        "Template argument deduction works from the argument types regardless of whether the parameter is T, T&, or T const& \u2014 the parameter form only controls how the argument binds. Fix it by header-only definitions; explicit instantiation (template int twice<int>(int);) works when the type set is closed and known.",
        "Reference parameters: a reference capture stores a reference to the local variable, not its value \u2014 it dangles exactly like a returned pointer to a local.",
        "Lambda captures: since C++14, auto in a lambda parameter list makes the call operator a function template; each distinct argument type triggers an instantiation. By-reference capture is safe only when the lambda is used within the captured variables' lifetime (e.g., passed to std::sort).",
        "Constexpr permits a function to be evaluated at compile time when called with constant expressions, and requires it to be usable there."
      ],
      "example": {
        "lang": "cpp",
        "code": "template <typename T>\nT const& max(T const& a, T const& b) { return a < b ? b : a; }  // references: no copies",
        "walkthrough": "Template argument deduction works from the argument types regardless of whether the parameter is T, T&, or T const& \u2014 the parameter form only controls how the argument binds. T const& binds without copying and accepts temporaries, which is why the standard library's generic algorithms take their arguments this way."
      },
      "pitfalls": [
        "Common trap: compile-time computation requires template metaprogramming.",
        "Common trap: generic lambdas require C++20 concepts.",
        "Common trap: templates cannot deduce reference types.",
        "Common trap: concepts make templates run faster."
      ],
      "try_it": "Try it now in the Terminal tab \u2192"
    },
    {
      "domain_id": "build-toolchain",
      "title": "Build & Toolchain \u2014 Core concepts",
      "concepts": [
        "Key function: the compiler emits a class's vtable in the translation unit containing the definition of its first non-inline virtual function (the 'key function').",
        "One Definition Rule: modern CMake is target-based: target_include_directories (and target_link_libraries, target_compile_features) attach usage requirements to one target, with PRIVATE (build only), PUBLIC (build + consumers), and INTERFACE (consumers only) controlling propagation precisely.",
        "Principle: if that function is declared but never defined \u2014 commonly a forgotten virtual destructor \u2014 no TU emits the vtable and the link fails."
      ],
      "example": {
        "lang": "bash",
        "code": "clang++ -std=c++20 -fsanitize=address,undefined -fno-omit-frame-pointer -g -o tests *.cpp\n./tests  # aborts with symbolicated trace on first error",
        "walkthrough": "AddressSanitizer instruments memory accesses to catch heap/stack/global overflows, use-after-free, and leaks (with LeakSanitizer); UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer catches signed-integer overflow, bad shifts, misaligned access, and null dereference. Together at ~2x slowdown they are the highest-signal automated memory check available \u2014 far beyond what warnings alone can prove."
      },
      "pitfalls": [
        "Common trap: set the include path as an environment variable instead of in CMake.",
        "Common trap: static libraries cannot satisfy references from .cpp files.",
        "Common trap: merge all targets into one executable so the leak does not matter.",
        "Common trap: linkers cannot handle virtual functions."
      ],
      "try_it": "Try it now in the Terminal tab \u2192"
    },
    {
      "domain_id": "core-syntax",
      "title": "C++ \u2014 Core concepts",
      "concepts": [
        "RAII ties resource lifetime to object lifetime: acquire in the constructor, release in the destructor. Stack-allocated objects clean themselves up on every exit path \u2014 including exceptions \u2014 which is why manual new/delete is rare in modern code.",
        "Smart pointers encode ownership: unique_ptr for exclusive ownership (movable, never copied \u2014 use std::make_unique), shared_ptr for shared ownership via reference counting (use std::make_shared), and weak_ptr for non-owning back-references that break cycles.",
        "Move semantics transfer resources instead of copying them: an rvalue reference (T&&) parameter can steal the source's heap buffer and leave it in a valid-but-empty state. This makes returning large objects by value cheap and powers efficient standard containers.",
        "The STL gives you containers (vector, map/unordered_map, set), iterators (the glue between containers and algorithms), and algorithms (<algorithm>: sort, find_if, transform). Prefer algorithms over hand loops \u2014 they state intent and get the edge cases right.",
        "Templates are compile-time code generation: function and class templates are instantiated per type used, giving zero-overhead genericity. Templates are duck-typed at instantiation \u2014 errors surface where used, which is why concepts (C++20) exist to constrain them up front.",
        "Value semantics by default: C++ copies unless you say otherwise (references, std::move). The rule of zero (let the compiler generate special members when members manage themselves), three, and five governs when you must write copy/move operations and destructors yourself."
      ],
      "example": {
        "lang": "cpp",
        "code": "#include <memory>\n#include <vector>\n#include <string>\n\nstd::vector<std::string> load_names() {\n    auto names = std::make_unique<std::vector<std::string>>();\n    names->push_back(\"ada\");\n    names->push_back(\"grace\");\n    return *names;  // moves the vector out; unique_ptr frees nothing twice\n}\n\nint main() {\n    auto v = load_names();  // no copy of the strings\n    return v.size() == 2 ? 0 : 1;\n}",
        "walkthrough": "make_unique creates the vector with exclusive ownership \u2014 no raw new, no leak if push_back throws. Returning *names moves the vector's heap buffer into the caller's vector (no string copies), and the emptied unique_ptr's destructor safely deletes nothing."
      },
      "pitfalls": [
        "Common trap: shared_ptr cycles \u2014 back-pointers must be weak_ptr or objects never die.",
        "Common trap: using a moved-from object as if it still held its value \u2014 it is valid but unspecified; reassign before use.",
        "Common trap: erasing from a container while iterating without using erase's return value \u2014 iterator invalidation.",
        "Common trap: `std::move` doesn't move anything by itself \u2014 it just casts to an rvalue; the move constructor does the work."
      ],
      "try_it": "Work through this in the Labs tab.",
      "domain_id_remapped_from": "cpp",
