  "resources": {
    "cheatsheet": {
      "title": "C++ Programming \u2014 Cheat Sheet",
      "sections": [
        {
          "heading": "Core Syntax",
          "items": [
            "Most vexing parse \u2014 C++ grammar prefers to parse Widget w(); as a function declaration (returning Widget, taking no parameters) rather than a variable definition.",
            "Uniform initialization \u2014 list initialization (braces) makes narrowing conversions \u2014 those that can lose information, like double\u2192int \u2014 ill-formed. Brace initialization Widget w{} unambiguously defines an object \u2014 and additionally forbids narrowing conversions.",
            "List initialization \u2014 parenthesized and = initialization permit them silently.",
            "Common trap \u2014 const references cannot call any member functions.",
            "Common trap \u2014 uniform initialization only works for aggregates.",
            "Common trap \u2014 auto cannot deduce numeric types in loops.",
            "Common trap \u2014 the first declared overload always wins ties.",
            "A using-directive in a header is transitive \u2014 everyone who includes it (directly or indirectly) gets the entire namespace injected, inviting collisions like a local count vs std::count and silently changing overload resolution. Keep using-directives in .cpp files after all includes, or qualify with std::."
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "OOP in C++",
          "items": [
            "Virtual destructors \u2014 = 0 declares a pure virtual function with no required implementation in the base, making the class abstract \u2014 it can be used as a pointer/reference type but never instantiated.",
            "Polymorphic deletion \u2014 rule: any class designed as a base with polymorphic use needs a virtual destructor \u2014 often virtual ~Base() = default.",
            "Object slicing \u2014 copying a derived object into a base-typed variable slices off everything beyond the base subobject, including the dynamic type \u2014 virtual calls then resolve to Base. While the base constructor runs, the derived members have not been constructed \u2014 the object\u2026",
            "Passing \u2014 Passing by (const) reference or pointer keeps the complete object and its dynamic type intact. The fix: pass needed data as constructor parameters, or use a post-construction init step.",
            "Common trap \u2014 setup() must be pure virtual for dispatch to work in constructors.",
            "Common trap \u2014 virtual functions cannot be redeclared in derived classes.",
            "Common trap \u2014 the access specifier only affects data members, not functions.",
            "Common trap \u2014 the base pointer must be cast to Derived* for virtual dispatch.",
            "Copying \u2014 Copying a derived object into a base-typed variable slices off everything beyond the base subobject, including the dynamic type \u2014 virtual calls then resolve to Base. Slicing is silent and compiles without warnings."
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "STL Containers",
          "items": [
            "Iterator invalidation \u2014 algorithms operate on iterator ranges, not containers \u2014 std::remove cannot change v's size, so it compacts the kept elements to the front and returns the new logical end.",
            "Map \u2014 :operator[] is specified to insert a value-initialized element when the key is absent \u2014 convenient for counting, surprising for lookups. Ordered map needs only operator<; unordered_map needs a hash (to pick a bucket) and equality (to resolve collisions).",
            "Erase-remove idiom \u2014 the erase-remove idiom, v.erase(std::remove(...), v.end()), erases the leftover tail.",
            "Common trap \u2014 operator[] throws std::out_of_range on missing keys.",
            "Common trap \u2014 a vector iterator cannot be dereferenced twice.",
            "Common trap \u2014 std::sort requires a total order.",
            "Common trap \u2014 std::remove requires a sorted range.",
            "Algorithms operate on iterator ranges, not containers \u2014 std \u2014 :remove cannot change v's size, so it compacts the kept elements to the front and returns the new logical end. (C++20's std::erase(v, value) does both in one call.)."
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Memory & Smart Pointers",
          "items": [
            "Exception safety \u2014 RAII ties resource release to object lifetime: the lock_guard's destructor runs when the scope exits for any reason \u2014 return, exception, or fall-through \u2014 so the mutex cannot be left locked.",
            "Move semantics \u2014 std::move is a cast to an rvalue reference; the move constructor then steals the source's resources. (unique_lock adds deferred/try semantics when needed.).",
            "Shared_ptr \u2014 Shared_ptr destroys its object when the last owner goes away, but in a cycle every object has an owner inside the cycle \u2014 the count never drops to zero. weak_ptr holds a non-owning reference: it can lock() to a temporary shared_ptr when access is needed, an\u2026",
            "Common trap \u2014 std::move destroys the source.",
            "Common trap \u2014 wrap the call in try/catch and delete manually in the catch block.",
            "Common trap \u2014 scalar delete works for arrays of trivial types.",
            "Common trap \u2014 delete on arrays leaks the count prefix.",
            "The \u2014 The compiler may evaluate new Widget, then priority(), then the unique_ptr constructor \u2014 if priority() throws in between, the raw pointer is lost and leaks. std::make_unique performs the allocation directly into the smart pointer's control path, eliminating\u2026"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Templates & Lambdas",
          "items": [
            "Template \u2014 Template argument deduction works from the argument types regardless of whether the parameter is T, T&, or T const& \u2014 the parameter form only controls how the argument binds. Fix it by header-only definitions; explicit instantiation (template int twice<int>\u2026",
            "Reference parameters \u2014 a reference capture stores a reference to the local variable, not its value \u2014 it dangles exactly like a returned pointer to a local.",
            "Lambda captures \u2014 since C++14, auto in a lambda parameter list makes the call operator a function template; each distinct argument type triggers an instantiation. By-reference capture is safe only when the lambda is used within the captured variables' lifetime (e.g., passed\u2026",
            "Constexpr \u2014 Constexpr permits a function to be evaluated at compile time when called with constant expressions, and requires it to be usable there.",
            "Common trap \u2014 compile-time computation requires template metaprogramming.",
            "Common trap \u2014 generic lambdas require C++20 concepts.",
            "Common trap \u2014 templates cannot deduce reference types.",
            "Common trap \u2014 concepts make templates run faster.",
            "Template \u2014 Template argument deduction works from the argument types regardless of whether the parameter is T, T&, or T const& \u2014 the parameter form only controls how the argument binds. T const& binds without copying and accepts temporaries, which is why the standard\u2026"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Build & Toolchain",
          "items": [
            "Key function \u2014 the compiler emits a class's vtable in the translation unit containing the definition of its first non-inline virtual function (the 'key function').",
            "One Definition Rule \u2014 modern CMake is target-based: target_include_directories (and target_link_libraries, target_compile_features) attach usage requirements to one target, with PRIVATE (build only), PUBLIC (build + consumers), and INTERFACE (consumers only) controlling propagat\u2026",
            "Principle \u2014 if that function is declared but never defined \u2014 commonly a forgotten virtual destructor \u2014 no TU emits the vtable and the link fails.",
            "Common trap \u2014 set the include path as an environment variable instead of in CMake.",
            "Common trap \u2014 static libraries cannot satisfy references from .cpp files.",
            "Common trap \u2014 merge all targets into one executable so the leak does not matter.",
            "Common trap \u2014 linkers cannot handle virtual functions.",
            "AddressSanitizer \u2014 AddressSanitizer instruments memory accesses to catch heap/stack/global overflows, use-after-free, and leaks (with LeakSanitizer); UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer catches signed-integer overflow, bad shifts, misaligned access, and null dereference. Together at ~\u2026"
          ]
        }
