    "glossary": [
      {
        "term": "Widget",
        "def": "C++ grammar prefers to parse Widget w(); as a function declaration (returning Widget, taking no parameters) rather than a variable definition. Brace initialization Widget w{} unambiguously defines an object \u2014 and additionally forbids nar\u2026"
      },
      {
        "term": "PUBLIC",
        "def": "The access specifier on inheritance caps the accessibility of inherited members: private inheritance (the default for class, unlike struct) makes public/protected base members private in the derived class. Public inheritance models is-a;\u2026"
      },
      {
        "term": "Base",
        "def": "Deleting a derived object through a base pointer without a virtual destructor is undefined behavior; in practice the derived destructor (and member destruction) is skipped, leaking resources. Rule: any class designed as a base with polym\u2026"
      },
      {
        "term": "CMake",
        "def": "One Definition Rule: modern CMake is target-based: target_include_directories (and target_link_libraries, target_compile_features) attach usage requirements to one target, with PRIVATE (build only), PUBLIC (build + consumers), and INTERF\u2026"
      },
      {
        "term": "Who",
        "def": "Who else is affected by a using-directive at the top of a header \u2014 just that header, or everyone who includes it?"
      },
      {
        "term": "Template",
        "def": "Template argument deduction works from the argument types regardless of whether the parameter is T, T&, or T const& \u2014 the parameter form only controls how the argument binds. Fix it by header-only definitions; explicit instantiation (tem\u2026"
      },
      {
        "term": "Brace",
        "def": "Uniform initialization: list initialization (braces) makes narrowing conversions \u2014 those that can lose information, like double\u2192int \u2014 ill-formed. Brace initialization Widget w{} unambiguously defines an object \u2014 and additionally forbids\u2026"
      },
      {
        "term": "Virtual",
        "def": "While the base constructor runs, the derived members have not been constructed \u2014 the object's dynamic type is effectively the base. Virtual dispatch therefore resolves within the base."
      },
      {
        "term": "By-reference",
        "def": "Lambda captures: since C++14, auto in a lambda parameter list makes the call operator a function template; each distinct argument type triggers an instantiation. By-reference capture is safe only when the lambda is used within the captur\u2026"
      },
      {
        "term": "Map",
        "def": "Map::operator[] is specified to insert a value-initialized element when the key is absent \u2014 convenient for counting, surprising for lookups. Ordered map needs only operator<; unordered_map needs a hash (to pick a bucket) and equality (to\u2026"
      },
      {
        "term": "Principle",
        "def": "Principle: if that function is declared but never defined \u2014 commonly a forgotten virtual destructor \u2014 no TU emits the vtable and the link fails."
      },
      {
        "term": "Rule",
        "def": "Deleting a derived object through a base pointer without a virtual destructor is undefined behavior; in practice the derived destructor (and member destruction) is skipped, leaking resources. Rule: any class designed as a base with polym\u2026"
      },
      {
        "term": "Move",
        "def": "Move semantics: std::move is a cast to an rvalue reference; the move constructor then steals the source's resources. (unique_lock adds deferred/try semantics when needed.)."
      },
      {
        "term": "Object",
        "def": "Object slicing: copying a derived object into a base-typed variable slices off everything beyond the base subobject, including the dynamic type \u2014 virtual calls then resolve to Base. While the base constructor runs, the derived members ha\u2026"
      },
      {
        "term": "Lambda",
        "def": "Lambda captures: since C++14, auto in a lambda parameter list makes the call operator a function template; each distinct argument type triggers an instantiation. By-reference capture is safe only when the lambda is used within the captur\u2026"
      },
      {
        "term": "Overload",
        "def": "Overload resolution ranks implicit conversion sequences: exact match first, then integral/floating promotions, then other standard conversions (int\u2192long), and only then user-defined conversions (via a converting constructor to std::strin\u2026"
      },
      {
        "term": "Overriding",
        "def": "Overriding requires an identical signature (plus compatible return types). draw(long) is a different function that hides the base name instead of overriding it."
      },
      {
        "term": "Polymorphic",
        "def": "Polymorphic deletion: rule: any class designed as a base with polymorphic use needs a virtual destructor \u2014 often virtual ~Base() = default."
      },
      {
        "term": "TUs",
        "def": "The one-definition rule allows only one definition of a non-inline function program-wide. inline explicitly permits the same definition in multiple TUs (the linker merges them) \u2014 it is the mechanism for header-defined helpers, and modern\u2026"
      },
      {
        "term": "Call",
        "def": "A vector stores elements contiguously; when push_back exceeds capacity it allocates a bigger buffer and moves everything, leaving old iterators dangling. The rule: any operation that may reallocate (push_back, insert, reserve beyond capa\u2026"
      },
      {
        "term": "Wall",
        "def": "-Werror elevates every warning to a build failure, so no warning can scroll past unnoticed \u2014 the codebase stays warning-clean by construction. -Wall -Wextra enable the high-signal diagnostics (sign compare, unused, shadowing); -Wconversi\u2026"
      },
      {
        "term": "Parent",
        "def": "shared_ptr destroys its object when the last owner goes away, but in a cycle every object has an owner inside the cycle \u2014 the count never drops to zero. weak_ptr holds a non-owning reference: it can lock() to a temporary shared_ptr when\u2026"
      },
      {
        "term": "Wextra",
        "def": "-Werror elevates every warning to a build failure, so no warning can scroll past unnoticed \u2014 the codebase stays warning-clean by construction. -Wall -Wextra enable the high-signal diagnostics (sign compare, unused, shadowing); -Wconversi\u2026"
      },
      {
        "term": "Slicing",
        "def": "Copying a derived object into a base-typed variable slices off everything beyond the base subobject, including the dynamic type \u2014 virtual calls then resolve to Base. Passing by (const) reference or pointer keeps the complete object and i\u2026"
      },
      {
        "term": "Calling",
        "def": "While the base constructor runs, the derived members have not been constructed \u2014 the object's dynamic type is effectively the base. Virtual dispatch therefore resolves within the base."
      }
